  "limitations": [
    "Two reversed-order repetitions compare the original normalizer with a checked fast path for already canonical sources. Both copy the batch seed and retain full live digests, acquisition budgets, checks and append export.",
    "Every fast-path input is scanned for literal types, strict literal/clause order, no repeated variables, no tautologies, empty-clause policy and compact positive variable support. All other inputs use the original normalizer. Keys, certificates and recognition policy are unchanged.",
    "Both methods construct a fresh checked inventory and complete maintained catalog, query all originals, then resume the same pending split with at most 32 admitted states and twelve advances. Updated global queries follow the new evidence.",
    "The fast path retains no normalization cache. Transient tuples and support sets, serialization, fallback scans, certificate checking and cold replay remain costs. Counts are logical work measures, not peak RAM.",
    "Shared parent restoration, final cold global coverage, complete catalog audits and reference evidence are outside the matched sequence and separately charged. Two development-case observations share OS caches and provide no held-out or asymptotic SAT claim.",
    "No minimum-expansion or unrestricted polynomial-SAT bound follows."
  ],
